# Module 1 — Lecture 1: History & Approaches in Cognitive Neuroscience

> [!info] 4 sources · 32 RAG chunks · Key exam topics marked with bold headers

---

## 1. What is Cognitive Neuroscience?

Cognitive neuroscience is defined as work at the **intersection of cognitive science
and neuroscience**. It seeks to understand the neural basis of cognition using every
tool available: brain scanners, lesion studies, brain perturbations, and computational
modelling.

~~~voxi
CANVAS 660 260 #0f1117
-- Background panels
RECT 40 30 240 200 1 #1a2a4a 12
RECT 380 30 240 200 1 #0f2a1a 12

-- Left: Cognitive Science
TEXT 80 50 "COGNITIVE" 20 3 #6c8cff bold
TEXT 80 76 "SCIENCE" 20 3 #6c8cff bold
TEXT 60 110 "Mental processes:" 11 3 #8ec5fc
TEXT 60 128 "perception, attention," 10 3 #8888a0
TEXT 60 144 "memory, language," 10 3 #8888a0
TEXT 60 160 "decision-making" 10 3 #8888a0
TEXT 60 186 "Can ignore the brain" 9 3 #5a6a8a

-- Right: Neuroscience
TEXT 410 50 "NEURO-" 20 3 #43a047 bold
TEXT 410 76 "SCIENCE" 20 3 #43a047 bold
TEXT 400 110 "Nervous system:" 11 3 #81c784
TEXT 400 128 "organisation, function," 10 3 #8888a0
TEXT 400 144 "ion channels, cells," 10 3 #8888a0
TEXT 400 160 "circuits, signalling" 10 3 #8888a0
TEXT 400 186 "Can ignore cognition" 9 3 #5a8a6a

-- Center: Intersection
ELLIPSE 330 130 80 80 2 #1a1a3a
RECT 270 80 120 100 4 #2a1a3a 12
TEXT 282 100 "COGNITIVE" 14 5 #e040fb bold
TEXT 274 118 "NEURO-" 14 5 #e040fb bold
TEXT 274 136 "SCIENCE" 14 5 #e040fb bold
TEXT 280 162 "Grounding" 9 5 #ce93d8
TEXT 280 176 "mind in brain" 9 5 #ce93d8

-- Arrows
LINE 260 130 280 130 3 #6c8cff 2
LINE 390 130 380 130 3 #43a047 2
~~~

**Key distinction:** Unlike pure cognitive science (which can ignore the brain) or
molecular neuroscience (which studies ion channels), cognitive neuroscience tries to
**ground psychological constructs in neurobiological processes**.

> [!warning] Common Misconception
> Cognitive neuroscience is NOT simply about mapping brain regions activated during
> a task. The field goes well beyond creating maps of brain function — it aims to
> create neurobiologically grounded *models* of cognition.

---

