### 2.2 Behaviourism (Early 20th Century)

Driven by **B.F. Skinner** (1904-1990). Core argument: We cannot study mental
representations directly, so focus only on observable behaviour. The brain was
treated as a **"black box"** — only the stimulus-response relationship matters.

~~~voxi
CANVAS 660 110 #0f1117
-- The Black Box model
RECT 30 20 140 70 1 #43a047 6
TEXT 50 40 "STIMULUS" 14 2 #43a047 bold
TEXT 50 60 "Light, sound" 9 2 #81c784
TEXT 50 76 "Reinforcement" 9 2 #81c784

LINE 170 55 230 55 2 #8888a0 2
TEXT 182 40 "input" 9 3 #8888a0

RECT 230 15 180 80 1 #2d3244 6
TEXT 270 34 "BLACK BOX" 16 2 #e53935 bold
TEXT 264 58 "No internal states" 10 2 #ef9a9a
TEXT 264 74 "No mental models" 10 2 #8888a0

LINE 410 55 470 55 2 #8888a0 2
TEXT 420 40 "output" 9 3 #8888a0

RECT 470 20 160 70 1 #1e88e5 6
TEXT 490 40 "RESPONSE" 14 2 #1e88e5 bold
TEXT 490 60 "Lever press" 9 2 #90caf9
TEXT 490 76 "Behaviour" 9 2 #90caf9
~~~

### 2.3 The Cognitive Revolution (Mid-20th Century)

Born around the 1950s-60s at the **Dartmouth conference** on AI and Cognitive Science.
**Noam Chomsky** argued that behaviourism cannot explain the structural and generative
properties of language — humans have language capabilities animals do not share.

This challenged the S-R framework and revived the legitimacy of studying cognitive
functions. Eventually, the question arose: *How do we ground psychological constructs
in neurobiological processes?* — This gave birth to **Cognitive Neuroscience**.

---

